WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.068 --> 00:00:03.680
INTRO:
I’m sure you’ve all heard of Vladimir Putin. He’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:08.960
been the Tsar of Russia for the past 22 years,&nbsp;
and it doesn’t look like he’s going anywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:18.800
anytime soon. After all, his approval ratings are&nbsp;
over 80%. Now of course, I know a lot of you guys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:24.240
don’t believe these numbers, but these numbers&nbsp;
are still very telling. While these numbers may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:29.200
not be indicative of his public support, they&nbsp;
are indicative of his inner circle support.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.520
One of the main reasons that Putin is able to&nbsp;
get away with starting a war and jeopardizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:39.040
the Russian economy is because he has dozens of&nbsp;
rich oligarchs that back him up. In fact, these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:44.240
oligarchs were the ones that made Putin president&nbsp;
in the first place. And the most notable of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:50.320
oligarchs is ironically a man named Vladimir&nbsp;
Potanin. Vladimir Potanin is the richest man in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:57.440
all of Russia coming in at $31 billion. Aside from&nbsp;
his extraordinary fortune, Potanin was the former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:02.000
first deputy prime minister of Russia. And this&nbsp;
is the position that he got for making Yeltsin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:11.840
president. So, here’s the story of Vladimir&nbsp;
Potanin and how he made Putin the Tsar of Russia.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.440
SOVIET UNION:
Taking a look back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:21.600
Potanin was born on January 3, 1961 in Moscow.&nbsp;
This was during the height of the Cold War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:26.720
and the infamous Cuban Missile Crisis was just&nbsp;
around the corner. So, Potanin’s entire childhood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:32.160
was filled with communist propaganda and anti-US&nbsp;
sentiment. This was especially the case due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:37.280
his father Oleg. Oleg was a highly respected&nbsp;
Soviet diplomat who traveled internationally to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:42.640
meet with countries like New Zeland and Australia.&nbsp;
Meanwhile, Potanin’s mother was a doctor who often&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:48.000
accompanied Oleg on his business trips. This meant&nbsp;
that Potanin was often left with his grandparents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:54.240
Despite this, Potanin has always credited much&nbsp;
his wit and intelligence to his parents. Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.960
Potanin would end up following in his father’s&nbsp;
footsteps and enroll in the Moscow State Institute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:03.600
of International Relations. This university was&nbsp;
especially designed to prepare students to serve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:10.240
in the KGB and Kremlin sectors, and that’s exactly&nbsp;
what Potanin would do after graduating in 1983.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:15.360
His first job was at an arm of the Ministry&nbsp;
of Foreign Trade called FTO Soyuzpromexport.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.520
He would spend his entire 20s here as he&nbsp;
learned about the inner workings of the USSR and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:24.560
built up a professional network. Honestly, I’m&nbsp;
not sure if he would have left the Ministry at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:29.520
all if the USSR was still strong. You see, the&nbsp;
USSR would end up collapsing the same year that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:35.840
Potanin quit in 1991. So, it looks like Potanin&nbsp;
left sensing what was coming. Fortunately though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:39.840
he was able to make a lot of powerful&nbsp;
connections during his time at the Ministry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:45.040
and he would these connections to setup a private&nbsp;
association called Interros. On the surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:49.760
Interros was simply a trade association that&nbsp;
dealt with common metals such as aluminum, copper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:55.440
and lead. Russia has a decent supply of all these&nbsp;
metals, so it seemed like Potanin was looking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:00.800
build up a classic raw material business, but&nbsp;
this was not the case. Interros was simply a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:05.360
front to run his true money maker which was the&nbsp;
infamous financial scheme called loan for shares.

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:09.520
LOANS FOR SHARES:&nbsp;

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:14.480
Most of you are probably not familiar with loans&nbsp;
for shares because the scheme is unique to Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:20.560
and Potanin himself. In fact, Potanin was the&nbsp;
mastermind behind the scheme. It all started with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.520
opening up two financial institutions called the&nbsp;
International Company for Finance and Onexim Bank.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.520 --> 00:03:29.440
Potanin founded these two institutions with&nbsp;
a business partner named Mikhail Prokhorov&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:34.560
who is also a deca billionaire today. Anyway,&nbsp;
Potanin would be made president of the bank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:39.360
and the two would go onto recruit depositors.&nbsp;
This was actually super easy because they faced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:44.640
virtually no competition. You see, private&nbsp;
banking was banned in the USSR and the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:49.920
bank in the entire country was the central bank&nbsp;
called Gosbank. Potanin’s Onexim bank was one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:55.520
the first banks to pop up after the collapse of&nbsp;
the USSR. And given all of Potanin’s connections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:01.120
from the ministry, Onexim was able to become the&nbsp;
largest private bank in Russia in no time. And now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:06.480
that Potanin had money on his side, he started to&nbsp;
put this money to work. At the time, Russia didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:11.440
have a stock market or any prominent private&nbsp;
business. Basically everything was state owned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.600
so if Potanin wanted to gain ownership in&nbsp;
any of these businesses, he had to work with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:20.640
the government. Fortunately for Potanin, this was&nbsp;
actually super easy because Potanin had something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:25.840
that the government desperately needed: cash. At&nbsp;
the time, the president of Russia was a man named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.400
Boris Yeltsin who was up for reelection very soon.&nbsp;
Given that the the country was going through a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:35.920
massive liquidity crisis, his reelection prospects&nbsp;
were iffy to say the least. So, President Yeltsin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.160
would start looking for ways to raise cash&nbsp;
to save Russia as well as his presidency.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:44.880
And this is where Potanin would step in with his&nbsp;
loans for shares program. The idea was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.880 --> 00:04:49.680
super simple, he was basically just offering the&nbsp;
government margin loans. Potanin and his business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:54.000
partners would bail out the government and fund&nbsp;
Yeltsin’s reelection campaign if Yeltsin put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:59.360
up 12 state owned energy and mining companies as&nbsp;
collateral. Yeltsin would of course take the deal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:03.840
and Potanin would help Yeltsin win reelection.&nbsp;
However, this didn’t really help the Russian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.800
government change directions. The government was&nbsp;
still burning loads of money and Potanin’s money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.600
just allowed for a few more years of survival. It&nbsp;
wasn’t long until Russia was facing a liquidity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:19.280
crisis once again and this would eventually&nbsp;
climax in the 1998 Russian Finanical Crisis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:23.760
During this crisis, Russia would default on their&nbsp;
debt which meant that Potanin and his friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:28.960
could keep the collateral. For most financial&nbsp;
institutions, this would be far from ideal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.440
They’d rather just get their money back than get&nbsp;
stock or real estate or whatever the collateral&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:38.960
may be. But, for Potanin and his friends, this&nbsp;
is exactly what they wanted. The loans that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:43.680
they gave Russia were extremely small relative to&nbsp;
the collateral that they had, so they had hit the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:51.840
jackpot. They had basically just margin called&nbsp;
Russia to become multi billionaires themselves.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:54.560
PUTIN:
After such a public failure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:58.960
I don’t think that you’d be surprised to hear&nbsp;
that Yeltsin lost all public support. There was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:03.680
no way for him to win yet another election, so&nbsp;
he would do the honorable thing and step aside.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:13.440
Right before his term ended in 2000, Yeltsin would&nbsp;
go ahead and resign and tap on Vladimir Putin to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:18.000
become acting president until the next election.&nbsp;
In the meantime, Potanin was having the time of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:23.680
his life. After Yeltsin’s initial victory, Potanin&nbsp;
was appointed as the First Deputy Prime Minister.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:28.800
However, Potanin didn’t stick around for long.&nbsp;
He would leave in March of 1997 as he could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:33.760
sense what was coming and he didn’t want to be&nbsp;
on that side of the crisis. Instead he spent his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:38.640
time building up companies that were valuable and&nbsp;
offloading ones that weren’t. For example, he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:43.760
keen on protecting his 54% stake in Nornickel&nbsp;
which was a nickel mining and smelting company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:47.120
Meanwhile, he offloaded positions in other&nbsp;
companies like Svayzinvest. Svyazinvest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:52.720
was a telecommunications giant in Russia, but&nbsp;
it seems that Potanin knew that it was a garbage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:57.120
company on the inside, so he started looking&nbsp;
for potential targets that he could sell his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:04.240
position to. He convinced Soros to invest $980&nbsp;
million into the company in return for a 25%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:09.760
stake. Soros would eventually realize that this&nbsp;
was the “worst investment” of his life. So far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:15.680
Potanin had left Yeltsin and Soros out to die, but&nbsp;
he wasn’t so careless with all his relationships.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:20.400
One relationship that he valued highly was his&nbsp;
friendship with Putin. It was clear to Potanin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:25.040
that Putin was gonna be in power for quite some&nbsp;
time, so it was in his best interest to stay on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:29.840
Putin’s good side and that’s exactly what he’s&nbsp;
done. According to the US Treasury, Putin’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:36.080
inner circle is made up of 210 inidivuals out of&nbsp;
which 96 are oligarchs. And given that Potanin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:40.480
is the richest of them all, there’s no doubt&nbsp;
that he has quite a bit of influence on Putin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:44.320
And now that Potanin had Putin’s ear, he&nbsp;
would return to his treacherous behavior.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:48.640
BACKSTABS:&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:54.000
One of the first people that Potanin would turn on&nbsp;
is Mikhail Prokhorov. If you don’t remember, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:58.560
was the guy that helped Potanin setup the Onexim&nbsp;
bank and eventually the loan for shares program.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.280
In 2007, Potanin announced that he was splitting&nbsp;
ways with Prokhorov and he would launch a bid to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:08.400
buyout his stake in Nornickel. He offered just&nbsp;
$1 billion for Prokhorov’s stake. According to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:13.600
Prokhorov, his 25% stake was worth $15&nbsp;
billion. Prokhorov would turn to Putin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:18.400
for support and Putin would actually side with&nbsp;
Prokhorov probably because Putin values loyalty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:24.080
over everything else and Potanin was clearly not&nbsp;
loyal. So, Putin would actually call Potanin and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:28.800
chew him out saying quote “ It’s dishonest to&nbsp;
cheat on partners.” Thanks to Putin’s support,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:32.640
Prokhorov would be able to sell his stake to&nbsp;
Oleg Deripaska instead who gave him a much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:38.880
reasonable $8 billion. This gave Prokhorov an&nbsp;
out from this fiasco, but Potanin was obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:45.520
not happy. He shifted his dispute over to&nbsp;
Deripaska and this lasted all the way till 2012.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:50.880
At this point, a man named Roman Abramovich&nbsp;
would buy a 6.5% stake in Nornickel and he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.880 --> 00:08:55.200
would basically become a peacemaker between the&nbsp;
two. This stopped the fighting between Potanin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.200 --> 00:09:01.280
and Daripaska, but now Potanin has brought the&nbsp;
fight over the Abramovich. In February of 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.280 --> 00:09:07.520
he offered to buy 4% from Abramovich. Clearly,&nbsp;
this guy doesn’t stop until he gets what he wants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:11.920
This is not that big of a problem when he’s&nbsp;
simply fighting other businessmen who are probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:16.720
just as ruthless. But, it becomes quite an issue&nbsp;
when his actions affect the environment as there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:21.360
no one to protect the environment. Historically,&nbsp;
Nornickel has been one of the largest polluters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:27.280
of the Russian Arctic. As of 2013, Nornickel was&nbsp;
discharging 500 tons of copper and nickel oxides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.280 --> 00:09:31.520
as well as 2 million tons of sulfor dioxide&nbsp;
every year. This pollution has reduced the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:36.480
life expectancy of people living in the area by&nbsp;
10 years relative to the rest of Russia. Putin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.480 --> 00:09:41.200
has been aksing Potanin to reduce his pollution&nbsp;
for quite some time, but Potanin has yet to take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.200 --> 00:09:47.840
any meaningful action. If anything, things have&nbsp;
only gotten. In fact, in May of 2020, Nornickel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:52.720
had an oil spill that flooded Russian rivers&nbsp;
with 21,000 tonnes of diesel. This was Russia’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:58.480
second largest oil spill of all time and it has&nbsp;
resulted in a $2 billion fine for Nornickel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:03.840
Hopefully, a financial punishment is enough to&nbsp;
change Potanin’s ways, but only time will tell.

00:10:03.840 --> 00:10:08.000
POTANIN TODAY:&nbsp;

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:12.720
Despite all of the bad deeds of Potanin has been&nbsp;
involved in, things have been going quite well for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:17.760
him. In fact, Potanin’s firm, Nornickel is one&nbsp;
of the biggest Russian firms that haven’t been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:22.560
sanctioned by the western world at large. Only&nbsp;
a few countries such as the UK and Canada have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:26.320
sanctioned off his company. The rest of the&nbsp;
world hasn’t really taken any action against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:30.960
Potanin though due to how important his company is&nbsp;
to the global metal industry. This is why Potanin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:34.400
is one of the only Russian billionaires&nbsp;
who hasn’t shed much wealth this year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:40.960
The rest of the Russian billionaires are down $5&nbsp;
to $10 billion since the beginning of the year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.960 --> 00:10:46.400
So, clearly, things have been playing out pretty&nbsp;
well for Potanin. Looking forward, it doesn’t seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:51.200
like his wealth or influence will be disappearing&nbsp;
anytime soon especially given that he’s not even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.200 --> 00:10:56.640
hurting due to the sanctions. So, all we can do&nbsp;
is hope that Potanin scales back his pollution and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:02.320
uses his influence for good instead of supporting&nbsp;
unnecessary wars. But that’s just what I think.&nbsp;&nbsp;

